WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.390
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.901
This is not some hot new Two Minute Papers
merchandise - this is what a new cloth simulation

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:13.839
paper is capable of doing today.

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:15.460
So good!

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:21.241
And today, we are also going to see how to
put back the holes into holey geometry, and

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:22.660
do it quickly!

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.450
But wait a second, that is impossible.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:31.310
We know that yarn-based cloth simulations
take forever to compute.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:37.380
Here is an example, we showcased this previous
work approximately 60 episodes ago.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:44.380
And curiously, get this, some of the simulations
here were nearly 60 times faster than the

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.260
yarn-based reference simulation.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.640
That is amazing.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:54.590
However, we noted that even though this technique
was a great leap forward, but of course, it

00:00:54.590 --> 00:01:00.590
wasn’t perfect, there was a price to be
paid for this amazing speed, which was, look,

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:05.600
that pulling effects on individual yarns were
neglected.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.460
That amazing holey geometry is lost.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.430
I noted that I’d love to get that back.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.770
You will see in a moment that maybe, my wish
comes true today.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:16.770
Hmm!

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.630
So, quick rundown.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.850
We either go for a mesh-based cloth simulation.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.590
These are really fast, but, no holey geometry.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.990
Or, we choose the yarn-level simulations,
these are the real deal, however, they take

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.010
forever.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:37.400
Unfortunately, still, there seems to be no
way out here.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.330
Now, let’s have a look at this new technique.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:48.790
It promises to try to marry these two concepts,
or in other words, use a fast mesh simulator,

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.610
and add the yarn-level cloth details on top
of it.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:58.810
Now of course, that is easier said than done,
so let’s see how this new method can deal

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.729
with this challenging problem.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:07.979
So, this is the fast mesh simulation to start
with, and now, hold on to your papers, and

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:10.270
let’s add those yarns.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.440
Oh my, that is beautiful.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.230
I absolutely love how at different points,
you can even see through these garments.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.230
Beautiful.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.560
So, how does it do all this magic?

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.830
Well, look at this naive method.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:32.050
This neglects the proper tension between the
yarns, and, look at how beautifully they tighten

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:35.160
as we add the new method on top of it.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.930
This technique can do it properly.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:43.300
And not only that, but it also simulates how
the garment interacts with a virtual body

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.260
in a simulated world.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:53.750
Once again, note how the previous, naive method
neglects the tightening of the yarns.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:59.410
So, I am super excited, let’s see how long
we have to wait for this.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:06.090
Are we talking fast mesh simulation timings,
or slow yarn simulation timings?

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.790
My goodness.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.060
Look at that!

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:15.390
The mesh part runs on your processor, while
the yarn part of the simulation is implemented

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.140
on the graphics card.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:25.000
And, whoa, the whole thing runs in the order
of milliseconds, easily in real time.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.709
Even if we have a super detailed garment with
tens of millions of vertices in the simulation,

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.670
the timings are in the order of tens of milliseconds
at worst.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.420
That’s also super fast.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:39.420
Wow.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.870
So, yes, part of this runs on our graphics
card and in real time.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.940
Here, we have nearly a million vertices, not
a problem.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:55.950
Here, nearly two million vertices, two million
points if you will, it runs like a dream.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:01.470
And it only starts to slow down when we go
all the way to a super detailed piece of garment

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.670
with 42 million vertices.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:10.770
And here, there is so much detail that finally,
not the simulation technique is the bottleneck,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.440
but the light simulation algorithm is.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:19.609
Look, there are so many high-frequency details
that we would need a higher-resolution video,

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:24.779
or more advanced antialiasing techniques to
resolve all this detail.

00:04:24.779 --> 00:04:29.360
All this means that the simulation technique
did its job really well.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.830
So, finally, yarn-level simulations in real
time.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.349
What a time to be alive!

00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:42.089
Huge congratulations to the entire team, and
the first author, Georg Sperl, who is still

00:04:42.089 --> 00:04:45.280
a PhD student, making important contributions
like this.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:52.949
And, get this - Georg’s short presentation
was seen by… oh my…61 people.

00:04:52.949 --> 00:04:55.139
Views are not everything.

00:04:55.139 --> 00:04:56.319
Not even close!

00:04:56.319 --> 00:05:01.409
But, once again, if we don’t talk about
this work here, I am worried that almost no

00:05:01.409 --> 00:05:02.470
one will.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.169
And this is why Two Minute Papers exists.

00:05:05.169 --> 00:05:10.229
Subscribe if you wish to see more of these
miracle papers, we have some great ones coming

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:43.849
up.

00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:52.279
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:05:52.279 --> 00:05:52.870
and I'll see you next time!

